&lt;p&gt;The Diffie-Hellman key exchange is based on the following procedure:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Choose a large prime number $p$ and a base $g$ that is a primitive root modulo $p$.&lt;br&gt;
Each of the two users, Alice and Bob, generate a private key $a$ and $b$ respectively, where $a, b \lt p$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alice and Bob compute their public keys $A$ and $B$ respectively, where $A = g^a \bmod p$ and $B = g^b \bmod p$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Alice and Bob exchange their public keys. Each of them can compute the shared secret key $s$, where $s = B^a \bmod p$ or $s = A^b \bmod p$.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There exist values of $a$ and $b$ such that $g^{ab} \bmod p = g$.&lt;br&gt;We call these keys $a$ and $b$ for which $g^{ab} \bmod p = g$ unconcealed keys.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An issue when choosing $a$ and $b$ is that there should not be too many unconcealed keys.&lt;br&gt;For instance, let $p = 23$ and $g = 5$.&lt;br&gt;
If we choose $a = 6$ and $b = 15$, then, although $a, b \lt p$ it turns out that all possible keys $a$ and $b$ ($1 \le a, b \lt p$) are unconcealed when calculating $g^{ab} \bmod p$.&lt;br&gt;
For any valid choice of $a$ and $b$ there exist some unconcealed keys.&lt;br&gt;
It&#39;s important that the number of unconcealed keys is at a minimum.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Choose $p = 541$ and $g = 10$.&lt;br&gt;
Find the sum of all pairs of values of $a$ and $b$, $1 \lt a, b \lt p$, so that the number of unconcealed keys for these values of $a$ and $b$ is at a minimum.&lt;/p&gt;

