```php
class Validator
{
    private $rules = [];

    public function setRules($attribute, $rules)
    {
        $this->rules[$attribute] = $rules;
    }

    public function validate($data)
    {
        $errors = [];

        foreach ($this->rules as $attribute => $rules) {
            foreach ($rules as $rule => $message) {
                if ($rule === 'max') {
                    if (is_numeric($data[$attribute]) && $data[$attribute] > $message) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = str_replace(':max', $message, $message);
                    } elseif (is_string($data[$attribute]) && strlen($data[$attribute]) > $message) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = str_replace(':max', $message, $message);
                    } elseif (is_array($data[$attribute]) && count($data[$attribute]) > $message) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = str_replace(':max', $message, $message);
                    }
                } elseif ($rule === 'regex') {
                    if (!preg_match($message, $data[$attribute])) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = $message;
                    }
                } elseif ($rule === 'required') {
                    if (empty($data[$attribute])) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = $message;
                    }
                } elseif (strpos($rule, 'custom') === 0) {
                    $customRule = explode('.', $rule);
                    if (isset($data[$attribute]) && $data[$attribute] === $customRule[1]) {
                        $errors[$attribute] = $message;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return $errors;
    }
}

// Example usage
$validator = new Validator();
$validator->setRules('age', [
    'max' => 18,
    'required' => 'Age is required',
]);
$validator->setRules('email', [
    'regex' => '/^\S+@\S+\.\S+$/',
]);
$validator->setRules('customAttribute', [
    'custom.rule-name' => 'Custom error message for custom rule',
]);

$data = [
    'age' => 20,
    'email' => 'invalid-email',
    'customAttribute' => 'some-value',
];

$errors = $validator->validate($data);
print_r($errors);
```

In this solution, the `Validator` class is implemented with the ability to set validation rules, validate input data, and provide custom error messages based on the defined rules. The `validate` method iterates through the defined rules and checks the input data against each rule, generating custom error messages when validation fails. The example usage demonstrates how to set rules and validate input data using the `Validator` class.

