ht.insert("hello", "world")
print(ht.retrieve("hello"))  # Outputs: world
ht.delete("hello")
print(ht.retrieve("hello"))  # Outputs: None
```

Managed C++ (or C++/CLI) has a `pin_ptr` feature which can be used to fix managed object in memory and get a native pointer to its contents. The `interior_ptr` you have here is a managed pointer, pointing to something within a managed object. 

It's worth noting that `pin_ptr` prevents the .NET's Garbage Collector (GC) from moving the object in memory while the pointer variable is in scope. This is essential when we want to pass the address of a managed object to a native C++ function or library.

You can try the following in order to pass your void pointer to an unmanaged function. 

```C++
{
    pin_ptr<void*> pinned = &m_pStuff;
    oldFunction( reinterpret_cast<void**>( pinned ) );
}
```

`pin_ptr<void*>` pins the `cli::interior_ptr` and then you can safely reinterpret it as a `void**`. The extra {} braces will ensure the pinned pointer will go out of scope as soon as you don't need to have the object pinned, allowing the GC to do its job. 

This assumes `oldFunction` does not keep that pointer and use it asynchronously after it returns. If it does, your pinned pointer must stay in scope the entire time the old function might be using it.

