```rust
fn decode_cyclic(e: &str) -> String {
    // The given string is first divided into groups of three characters, but it respects the string length and does not access an index that does not exist.
    let mut groups: Vec<&str> = (0..).step_by(3).map(|i| &e[i..(i+3).min(e.len())]).collect();

    // The groups are then rearranged so that the last character of each three-character group is moved to the front. If the group does not have three characters (i.e. it's from the end of the string), it remains unchanged.
    groups.iter_mut().for_each(|group| {
        if group.len() == 3 {
            let last = group.pop().unwrap();
            group.insert(0, last);
        }
    });

    // The rearranged groups are then joined back into one single string.
    groups.concat()
}
```
Please note that Rust's ownership rules require a different approach to string slicing compared to Python. Also, Rust String does not have pop and insert methods, hence we consider full &str partitions as groups which will be sliced and indexed instead. The translated function will fail in case input is not UTF-8 encoded string, in such scenario consider using bytes instead of &str.

While translating this to Rust, please ensure the string e is fully UTF-8 encoded and doesn't have any character that may exceed the 3-byte limit. Characters like Emojis or Complex script characters may take more than 3 bytes per character. In such cases, use grapheme clusters for splitting and iterating through strings.

